    "text": "# `merge_intervals` \u2014 Merging Overlapping Closed Integer Intervals\n\n```python\ndef merge_intervals(intervals):\n    \"\"\"\n    Merge overlapping closed integer intervals.\n\n    Args:\n        intervals: iterable of (start, end) pairs, inclusive on both ends.\n\n    Returns:\n        A new sorted list of merged (start, end) tuples.\n        The input is not mutated.\n    \"\"\"\n    if not intervals:\n        return []\n\n    # Copy + sort by start, then by end (stable, deterministic).\n    ordered = sorted((tuple(iv) for iv in intervals), key=lambda iv: (iv[0], iv[1]))\n\n    merged = [ordered[0]]\n    for start, end in ordered[1:]:\n        last_start, last_end = merged[-1]\n        # Closed intervals overlap or touch when start <= last_end + 1.\n        if start <= last_end + 1:\n            if end > last_end:\n                merged[-1] = (last_start, end)\n        else:\n            merged.append((start, end))\n\n    return merged\n```\n\n## The Invariant\n\nAt the top of each loop iteration, the list `merged` satisfies:\n\n> **`merged` is sorted by start, its intervals are pairwise non-overlapping and non-adjacent (i.e., `merged[i].end + 1 < merged[i+1].start`), and every interval seen so far in `ordered` is fully contained in the union of `merged`.**\n\nTwo consequences make the algorithm correct:\n\n1. **Only the",
